# tools/s/shared_pen_witness.rish -- a scratch path named by a constant is a path two ships share.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. Eight ships run one roster from eight checkouts. A guard that builds its fixture in
# `/tmp/<a name somebody chose>` builds it in the same directory on every one of them, so two ships
# arriving within one guard's runtime read each other's state as their own. The reading that follows
# is always a fact about the pen wearing the costume of a fact about the subject.
#
# It has now fired three times in two laps, and each time the witness was GREEN when run alone:
# `signal_trap` and `tlb_reach` on `20260906.213507`, and `amphora_restore_negative` on this pier's
# cold pass at `20260906.222342`, whose assertion read `tamper refusal must leave no out-home` while
# a peer's `rm -rf /tmp/amphora_rneg_home` was the only thing that had touched it. Green alone and
# red in company is the signature. A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom.
#
# WHY THE ELDER SWEEP DID NOT CLOSE IT. The lap that repaired the first two counted the class by one
# spelling -- a shell `TMPDIR=` assignment -- and repaired nine files. This tree writes a pen at
# least FIVE ways: that assignment, a bare literal in a command, a Rishi `let home = "/tmp/name"`,
# a redirection target, and `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/<name>`, whose text holds no literal `/tmp/` at all.
# `amphora_restore_negative` was the third spelling and `tlb_reach_census.sh` the fifth, so a sweep
# passed over each in turn and the count of nine was a count of what one grep could see.
#
# THE FIFTH SPELLING IS WHY THIS GUARD REDDENED ITS FOURTH GUARD RATHER THAN CLOSING THE CLASS.
# `tlb_reach` reddened the cold pass of `20260906.233225` at *the probe no longer builds*, GREEN
# alone one minute later, and the cause was `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tlb_reach_build_pen` wiped with
# `rm -rf` before a Zig build. Two concurrent copies of the elder file: one of two failed with
# `compile_failed`. Two concurrent copies of the repaired file: both green. Proven on metal, both
# directions, `20260907.000549`.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, on metal.
#   The tree's constant-pen population, read across every spelling, under a ceiling that only falls.
#   The WIPING subset read separately, because a file that only writes a shared pen may interleave
#     and survive, while one that REMOVES it takes a peer's in-flight state down. Sixteen today,
#     and the number moved because the reading did: until `20260907.163000` the predicate demanded
#     an `r` or `R` flag, so `rm -f /tmp/name.txt` on a plain FILE read as a hold -- and the hold
#     class was defined by that survive-an-interleave claim, which was false of exactly those
#     files. `tools/fixtures/s/shipped_binary_claim_scan.sh` truncated a constant name, appended,
#     counted and removed it, and read RED in company and GREEN alone on the cold pass of
#     `20260907.150519`, taking `standing_equipment` red behind it. Two files were repaired in that
#     lap and the other sixteen are named by `--list` for their owners, five of them the Caravan
#     poll family sharing ONE sentinel path. Two tightenings landed beside the widening so it could
#     not buy its number with noise: a `/tmp` path must begin at a BOUNDARY, and a removal is
#     charged to the path it NAMES within its own command segment. The elder reading was also
#     LINE-SCOPED, so a file that
#     assigned a pen on one line and `rm -rf`d it on another read as a hold, and two of the six
#     were invisible (REDS `%544`). The scan takes two passes now -- collect the variables holding
#     a constant pen, then read a wipe naming one of them as a wipe of that pen -- and it found
#     exactly the two files a hand-count had named, which is the corroboration worth having. The
#     paragraph that declined this repair declined it on a measurement taken by the reading it was
#     defending, which is the shape to remember rather than the two files.
#   THE POPULATION IS A CLASS RATHER THAN A ROOM, from `20260908.052557`. The source line read
#     `git ls-files 'tools/*'`, and a pen is contended because eight checkouts share one `/tmp` --
#     a reason that names no directory. The roster already ran files outside that room:
#     `tools/r/rish_join_split_witness.rish` runs three scripts under `rishi/tests/` and
#     `tools/fixtures/r/rishi_bounded_process_control.sh` runs six more, so the meter could not read
#     a room the roster executes. Widened to every tracked `.sh` and `.rish`, 3,221 sources to
#     3,289. It found exactly one file -- `rishi/tests/file_io.rish`, five sites, no wipe -- whose
#     own rostered sibling `tools/r/rish_file_io_witness.rish` had written into
#     `tools/fixtures/rish_io/` since it was seated; the elder copy never followed and nothing could
#     say so. Repaired to that anchor and run GREEN on metal in the same lap, so both ceilings stay
#     where they stood: 236 sites, 46 files, 15 wiping, before and after.
#   The population is a UNION rather than a suffix, from `20260908.072606`. A suffix is no more a
#     class than a directory was: a runner is a file the pier executes, and seven tracked runners
#     carry no suffix -- among them `tools/hooks/commit-msg`, `pre-commit` and `post-commit`, which
#     `core.hooksPath` runs on every ship at every commit, the exact instant REDS `%601` names. So
#     the reading is `.sh` or `.rish` OR a first-line shebang, read by `git grep -I -n` keeping
#     `lineno == 1`. Never a swap: 881 tracked files open with `#!` while 3,298 carry the suffixes,
#     and 2,424 of those 3,298 carry NO shebang, so a shebang-only reading would buy 7 and drop
#     2,424. Sources 3,298 -> 3,305; the seven hold no pen today, so both ceilings stay again.
#   Forty-eight behaviors on real git repositories in a throwaway pen -- both ceilings shown from
#     both sides, seven free rules each planted and read as zero, the quoting rule, the lock rule
#     and the variable tracker each shown in BOTH directions so none can be used as a hole, the
#     sixth spelling planted literal and held-in-a-name, both tightenings planted with the reading
#     they must not weaken, and two
#     refusals for a root that cannot answer, so an unreadable tree never reports clean. The count
#     is DERIVED by the control from its own claims rather than spelled, since it read 24 while 27
#     ran.
#
# TWICE THE CONTROL CAUGHT THIS GUARD COUNTING SOMETHING THE TREE HAD NOT DONE WRONG, and both are
# worth naming because both would have instructed a repair that was already made or never needed.
# The token class stops at `$`, so `/tmp/pen_$$` matched as the constant `/tmp/pen_` -- five files
# repaired the lap before, read as damage. And text inside single quotes is text: a control writing
# a plant into a throwaway tree, and a scan normalising a path bwrap made, are talking ABOUT a path
# rather than claiming one. Four files, seventeen sites, every one of them a control or a scan --
# two of them this guard's own control, so it was counting its plants (the shape %519 booked).
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. That any pen is ever actually contended, which depends on which ships run
# which guard at which second. This proves the shape that makes contention possible, and that is
# the whole of what a reading of the source can prove.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/s/shared_pen_witness.rish

say "shared-pen: Language EN -- a name chosen at write time is a name every ship chose."
say "shared-pen: Style Gauge, Meter -- refusal is the subject."
say "shared-pen: Lens TAME -- safety first: a guard reporting a peer's pen is a guard reporting nothing."

let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/shared_pen_scan.sh"]
assert scan.ok else "shared-pen: the constant-pen population stands past a ceiling -- read the scan's own lines"
say scan.out
assert scan.out contains "verdict=ok" else "shared-pen: the scan must balance before it is believed"
say "field GREEN -- the constant-pen population and its wiping subset both stand under their ceilings"

let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/shared_pen_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "shared-pen: the control must complete"
say control.out
assert control.out contains "clean_reads_zero=yes" else "shared-pen: a clean tree must read zero rather than merely pass"
assert control.out contains "clean_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a clean tree must pass free"
assert control.out contains "planted_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: a planted constant pen must be counted"
assert control.out contains "planted_named=yes" else "shared-pen: the reading must name the file, so the repair is one line away"
assert control.out contains "mktemp_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a mktemp line is unique by construction"
assert control.out contains "template_free=yes" else "shared-pen: an XXXXXX template is unique by construction"
assert control.out contains "pid_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a pen carrying the process id is already repaired"
assert control.out contains "interpolate_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a name chosen at run time is not a constant"
assert control.out contains "comment_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a comment creates no directory"
assert control.out contains "single_quoted_free=yes" else "shared-pen: text inside single quotes is text, not a path this file opens"
assert control.out contains "single_quoted_absent=yes" else "shared-pen: a freed token must be absent from the listing, not merely uncounted"
assert control.out contains "double_quoted_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: the quoting rule must not become a hole -- double quotes still count"
assert control.out contains "tmpdir_default_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: a pen written ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/<name> is a constant pen on a pier where TMPDIR is unset"
assert control.out contains "lock_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a lock over a pier-wide resource is shared on purpose"
assert control.out contains "lock_wiping_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: the lock rule must not become a hole -- a wiped .lock is a pen"
assert control.out contains "var_wipe_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: a pen held in a variable and wiped a line later is a wipe"
assert control.out contains "rishi_var_wipe_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: a Rishi list wiping a let-held pen is a wipe"
assert control.out contains "var_mktemp_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a variable named by mktemp holds no constant pen"
assert control.out contains "var_wipe_unheld_free=yes" else "shared-pen: the tracker must not read every wipe in a file that holds a pen as a wipe of it"
assert control.out contains "var_unheld_still_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: freeing the wipe must not free the pen"
# THE POPULATION IS EVERY RUNNER, and each Rye rule is bound here by NAME rather than by the
# tally alone -- a count is one assertion for eight behaviors, and a leg that quietly stops
# proving its own subject keeps the tally while losing the law (20260908).
assert control.out contains "rye_extension_read=yes" else "shared-pen: a Rye source is a runner and must be read"
assert control.out contains "outside_tools_read=yes" else "shared-pen: rooms outside tools/ hold runners too"
assert control.out contains "prose_not_a_runner=yes" else "shared-pen: prose names a path, it does not open one"
assert control.out contains "vendored_not_ours=yes" else "shared-pen: a vendored tree is held unmodified, never repaired here"
assert control.out contains "rye_comment_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a Rye comment teaches, it creates no directory"
assert control.out contains "rye_code_after_comment=yes" else "shared-pen: the comment rule must not become a hole"
assert control.out contains "rye_delete_is_a_wipe=yes" else "shared-pen: deleteTree removes a pen exactly as rm -rf does"
assert control.out contains "rye_hold_is_not_a_wipe=yes" else "shared-pen: a Rye pen merely held is a hold"
assert control.out contains "rye_runtime_name_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a bufPrint placeholder completes the name at run time"
assert control.out contains "rye_fixed_name_bites=yes" else "shared-pen: the run-time rule must not free a name fixed at write time"
assert control.out contains "behaviors=57" else "shared-pen: the control must run every behavior this witness names"
assert control.out contains "nonrecursive_wipe_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: a removal without an r flag still removes -- the sixth spelling"
assert control.out contains "nonrecursive_var_wipe_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: the sixth spelling must read through a held name too"
assert control.out contains "lock_release_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a lock is released with rm -f, so the widening must leave that rule standing"
assert control.out contains "device_tmp_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a path begins at a boundary -- /data/local/tmp is not this pier's /tmp"
assert control.out contains "separator_stops_the_wipe=yes" else "shared-pen: a removal is charged to the path it names, within its own command segment"
assert control.out contains "separator_still_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: freeing the wipe must not free the pen"
assert control.out contains "dated_testimony_free=yes" else "shared-pen: dated testimony keeps every word it wrote"
assert control.out contains "wiping_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: a wipe of a shared pen must be counted separately"
assert control.out contains "holding_not_wiping=yes" else "shared-pen: a pen only written to is not a pen wiped"
assert control.out contains "rishi_wipe_counted=yes" else "shared-pen: a Rishi list wipe is how this tree spells rm -rf"
assert control.out contains "files_at_ceiling_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a population at its ceiling must pass free"
assert control.out contains "files_over_ceiling_refused=yes" else "shared-pen: a population past its ceiling must refuse"
assert control.out contains "files_over_named=yes" else "shared-pen: the refusal must name a verdict, never only an exit code"
assert control.out contains "wipe_at_ceiling_free=yes" else "shared-pen: a wiping set at its ceiling must pass free"
assert control.out contains "wipe_over_ceiling_refused=yes" else "shared-pen: a wiping set past its ceiling must refuse"
# The elder line here read `other_extension_free` -- the law that a .rye source is NOT a runner.
# It was true of the scan and false of the tree, and rye/tests proved it false by racing on metal.
# The inverted legs are asserted by name above; this line stays as the record of what moved.
assert control.out contains "no_sources_refused=yes" else "shared-pen: a tree with no sources must refuse rather than read zero"
assert control.out contains "no_git_refused=yes" else "shared-pen: a root that is not a checkout must refuse"
assert control.out contains "control_verdict=proven" else "shared-pen: the control must agree with itself"
say "RED and GREEN paths proven -- four refusals bitten, every free rule planted then read as zero, and the quoting rule and the lock rule each shown from both sides"

say "GREEN: shared-pen -- every pen this guard counts is a pen two ships could both have claimed."
